{"kind":"stack","id":"stk-sep","title":"Separating mixtures: filtering, sieving and evaporating","theme":{"background":"#f4f9f4","foreground":"#1d3b2a","font":"Georgia, serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-t","content":"Filtering"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-e","content":"Filtering separates a solid that does not dissolve from a liquid, by pouring the mixture through paper or mesh that holds back the solid."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1-next","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1n-t","content":"Next Card"}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-q","content":"Which method would separate sand from water?"},{"type":"button","id":"c2-a","label":"Filtering","why":"Sand is a solid that does not dissolve, so filter paper holds it back.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-b","label":"Evaporating","why":"Evaporating only leaves behind something that dissolved, not sand.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-c","label":"Sieving the water","why":"Sieves hold back solids but water passes straight through.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"What is left behind when salty water evaporates?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"Salt","why":"Salt dissolved in the water, so it stays behind when the water evaporates.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"Nothing at all","why":"Pupils forget the salt is still there after the water is gone.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Water vapour only","why":"Pupils think the water is the only thing that remains, not the salt.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-t","content":"You have a bowl of gravel mixed with sand. Which way will you start?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"Sieve it","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"Add water and filter it","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-t","content":"The gravel stays in the sieve and the sand falls through. Well done, you chose wisely."},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"The sand dissolves in the water, so it passes through the filter with the water. Try again next time."},{"type":"button","id":"c6-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-q","content":"Which method best separates small stones from sand?"},{"type":"button","id":"c7-a","label":"Sieving","why":"A sieve with holes lets sand fall through while stones stay on top.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c7-b","label":"Evaporating","why":"Evaporating removes liquid, but sand and stones are both solids.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c7-c","label":"Adding water","why":"Adding water would not separate two solids that both stay solid.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-t","content":"Evaporating"},{"type":"text","id":"c8-e","content":"Evaporating separates a dissolved solid from a liquid by heating the liquid until it turns into gas and leaves the solid behind."}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8-next","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8n-t","content":"Next Card"}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"Which state of matter is water in when it evaporates?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"Gas","why":"Evaporating turns the liquid water into water vapour, which is a gas.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"Solid","why":"Pupils mix up freezing, which turns water solid, with evaporating.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"Liquid","why":"Pupils think it stays a liquid, but it has changed into a gas.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Which method would separate a mixture of salt and water?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"Evaporating","why":"Heating the salt water turns the water to gas and leaves the salt.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Sieving","why":"Salt dissolves, so it passes through a sieve with the water.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Filtering","why":"Salt dissolved in the water passes through filter paper with it.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-h","content":"Well done, you finished the separating mixtures quiz"},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"You now know how filtering, sieving and evaporating can separate the parts of a mixture."}],"actions":[]}}
